Abstract
Carbon nanotube-decorated nitrogen-doped carbon-coated nickel (CNT/Ni@N-C) microspheres have been fabricated by pyrolysis the mixture of nickel-based metal organic framework (Ni-MOF) and melamine. The resultant composite is assembled from CNTs and spherical $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{N-C}$ composite. Besides, the CNT/Ni@N-C composite contains abundant nitrogen (N) dopants that contribute to defect dipole polarization. The melamine content is important for controlling the amount of N element and N dopant state in composite. The optimized CNT/Ni@N-C composite with high content of pyridinic N exhibits strong absorption of -55.1 dB at 10.56 GHz with thickness of $2.5\mathrm{mm}$ , and the effective absorbing bandwidth (reflection loss $\mathrm{<-10~dB}$ ) is 11.2 GHz (6.0-17.2 GHz) with the thickness range of $1.5–4.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ . These results indicate that the N content and N dopant state have a great influence on electromagnetic wave absorption properties heavily. This strategy will contribute for fabricating the carbon hybrid network consisting of metal organic frameworks derived metal/carbon hybrid and CNTs for electromagnetic wave absorption.
Keywords: Metal organic framework, electromagnetic wave absorption, nickel, carbon nanotubes
## 1. Introduction
Recently, with the popularity of local area networks, radar systems, as well as the extensive application of computers, mobile phones and other electronic equipment, the problem of electromagnetic (EM) interference tends to be serious. Therefore, it is very important to effectively suppress and reduce EM radiation. The incident EM wave can be absorbed and attenuated by EM wave absorbing materials, which provides an effective solution to solve the EM wave pollution [1-3]. With the continuous development of research and application of EM absorbing materials, the type of materials tends to be diversified and complicated [4, 5]. The new EM absorbents are required to contain the characteristics of low thickness, lightweight, wide absorption frequency bandwidth and strong absorption performance [6, 7], which need extensive efforts to design and fabricate.
One-dimensional CNTs have unique hollow tubular structure, large aspect ratio, prominent electrical and mechanical properties [8, 9], which make them have a good application prospect in EM wave absorbing materials. However, the weak impedance matching limits the practical application of CNTs as EM wave absorbent [10, 11]. Therefore, combining CNTs with other materials is one of the effective strategies to get desirable EM wave absorbent. For instance, wang et al. prepared ternary composite of carbon $\mathrm{\langleFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/$ multi-wall carbon nanotubes with excellent microwave absorption behavior [12]. Wu’s group reported reduced graphene oxide/multi-walled carbon nanotubes/cerium oxide composite as absorber with maximum reflection loss (RL) of - 59.3 dB at $4.5\ \mathrm{mm}$ [13]. Zeng and co-workers fabricated necklace-like $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and carbon nanotube nanocomposite as microwave absorbing material and studied the effects of carbon nanotubes content on the microwave absorbing properties [14]. Nevertheless, the CNTs always require being pretreatment by complicated chemical process before they can be combined with other materials [15, 16]. Utilizing facile synthesis method to prepare the CNTs-based composites is still a challenge.
Currently, nonmetallic element doping have attracted tremendous attention for improving the dielectric loss of absorbers. It is worth noting that N doped carbon based materials commonly exhibit prominent microwave absorption performance, such as MoS2@N-doped carbon $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hybrid [17], Co@N-doped carbon composite [18], $\mathrm{Co}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}/\mathrm{N}.$ -doped mesoporous carbon sphere [19] and so on. The doped N element can introduce some defects which act as polarized centers for providing defect/dipole polarization under EM field. However, rare studies illustrate the effect of N content and N dopant state on microwave absorption performance.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) that consist of metal ions and organic ligands are appealing templates to fabricate efficient carbon based absorbers due to the advantages of tunable porosity and controllable microstructure [20]. Magnetic metal/carbon composites derived from MOFs inherit the morphology and the pore structure of original MOFs. Besides, magnetic particles are wrapped by carbon and uniformly dispersed in carbon matrix [21-23]. Especially in the field of EM absorption, the MOFs containing magnetic metal ions derived metal/carbon composites have both magnetic loss and electric loss, which provide multiple ways for the attenuation of EM waves [24]. Herein, in this work, Ni-MOF microsphere is used as the precursor, and melamine is used as initiator for the growth of CNTs and N supplier. After pyrolyzing the composite of melamine/Ni-MOF at argon atmosphere, CNT/Ni@N-C composite is obtained. The Ni-MOF microspheres are transformed into Ni-C composite and the generated Ni nanoparticles can also be used as the catalyst for CNTs growth. Both the N content and type can be regulated. The as-synthesized CNT/Ni@N-C composite with high amount of pyridinic N presents superior EM wave absorbing performances. The $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ is -55.1 dB at 10.56 GHz with the EAB of 11.2 GHz (6.0-17.2 GHz) at absorber thickness below $4.0\mathrm{mm}$ .
## 2. Experimental section
2.1 Materials. Benzene-1, 3, 5-tricarboxylic acid ( $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\mathrm{BTC}}$ , $98\%$ ), nickel nitrate hexahydrate $\mathrm{(Ni(NO_{3})_{2}{\bullet6}H_{2}O,98\%)}$ , ethanol $(99.7\%)$ ), polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP-K30, GR), melamine $(99\%)$ , and N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5~\%$ ) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd.
2.2 Synthesis of Ni-MOF. The Ni-MOF precursor was synthesized by solvothermal method similar to the previous report [25]. $864\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}.$ $300\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ and $3\mathrm{g}$ PVP-K30 were mixed with $60~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and stirred until the solution became light green transparent. Then it was sealed into Teflon-lined autoclave and maintained at $150~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ . The obtained green product was washed with ethanol and dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for overnight.
2.3 Synthesis of CNT/Ni@N-C composite. $400\mathrm{mg}$ of as-prepared Ni-MOF was mixed with various amounts of melamine $400\mathrm{mg},800\mathrm{mg}$ and $1200\mathrm{mg},$ ) in $100~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol and stirred for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ at $25~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ . After that, the suspension was dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in air until the solvent completely evaporated. Afterward, it was firstly pyrolyzed at $550^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ for 3 h and then subsequently at $700~^{\mathrm{{o}C}}$ for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ with a heating rate of $2~\mathrm{{^{o}C}~m i n^{-1}}$ and $3^{\mathrm{\oC}}$ $\mathrm{min^{-1}}$ , respectively. According to the different addition of melamine $(400\mathrm{mg},800\mathrm{mg}$ and $1200\ \mathrm{mg}$ ) the obtained CNT/Ni@N-C composites were recorded as CNT/Ni@NC-1, CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-2 and CNT/Ni@N-C-3, respectively. As a contrast, the Ni-MOF was also pyrolyzed at the same conditions, and it was named as Ni/C composite.
2.4 Characterization. The chemical composition of samples was investigated by Xray diffraction (XRD, D8 Advance, Germany). The morphology and structure of composites was examined by field-emission scanning electron microscope (FE-SEM, Hitachi, S-4800) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEM-2100). The surface composition of composites was performed using X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS, AXIS SUPRA). Raman spectra (Raman, DXRxi) was used to study the graphitization degree of samples.
2.5 Performance Measurements. The vector network analyzer (VNA, HP8720ES, Agilent, USA) was used to perform the complex permittivity $\pounds_{\mathrm{r}}\ (\pounds_{\mathrm{r}}=\pounds^{\prime}-j\varepsilon")$ and permeability $\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}\left(\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}=\upmu^{\prime}-j\upmu^{\prime\prime}\right)$ of products in the $2{\cdot}18\operatorname{GHz}$ of frequency range using the coaxial method. Before testing, the mixture containing $30\mathrm{\ut{\%}}$ the as-prepared materials and $70\mathrm{wt\%}$ wax was uniformly mixed at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and then pressed into toroidal ring (outer diameter: $7.0\mathrm{mm}$ , inner diameter: $3.04\mathrm{mm}$ and thickness: $3.0\mathrm{mm}$ ).
## 3. Results and discussion
The synthetic procedure of CNT/Ni@N-C composite is schematically shown in Scheme 1. The microspherical Ni-MOF is prepared by solvothermal reaction which is used as precursor to prepare CNT/Ni@N-C composites. The precursor is mixed with melamine and pyrolysed by two-stage programmed heating process. The melamine is transformed to $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ at $550~\mathrm{{^\circC}}$ for $3\mathrm{~h~}$ under argon atmosphere through its polyaddition and thermo-polymerization [26]. Then it permeates into the Ni-MOF microspheres. Finally, metal cations are reduced to metal nanoparticles and can be used as catalysts to facilitate the formation of CNTs after further increasing the temperature to $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $3\mathrm{{h}}$ . As a result, the CNT/Ni@N-C composite is obtained.
The morphology of composites are observed by FE-SEM. The Ni/C composite inherits the spherical structure of Ni-MOF precursor, as exposed in Figure 1(a). Without the melamine participating in the reaction, CNTs cannot be generated. SEM images of CNT/Ni@N-C composites are displayed in Figure 1(b-d). Obviously, the diameter and length of CNTs vary with the amount of melamine. CNT/Ni@N-C composites are composed of microspheres linked with plenty of flexible nanotubes.
As observed in TEM images of Figure 2(a-d), Ni-MOF has evolved into Ni/C composite with the initial spherical structure maintained, and nickel ions in Ni-MOF are transformed into nickel nanoparticles, which are embedded in carbon shell. During the pyrolysis process under argon atmosphere, PVP and organic ligands of $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC}$ are converted into carbon. The TEM images for representative sample of CNT/Ni@N-C-2 composite as shown in Figure 2(e-g). It consists of nickel carbon composite and CNT with Ni nanoparticles encapsulated in nanotubes. The generated Ni nanoparticles could be regarded as the catalyst to promote the conversion of melamine into CNTs. HRTEM images (Figure 2(h)) for a single Ni nanoparticle reveals a clear lattice fringes of $0.2~\mathrm{nm}$ , which corresponds to the (111) plane of cubic metallic Ni. Evidently, such
CNT/Ni@N-C composite with hierarchical hybrid nanoarchitectures may be beneficial to enhancing the EM wave transfer capability.
The phase identification of resultant CNT/Ni@N-C composites are performed via XRD patterns. As for Ni/C composite, peaks appeared at about $2\uptheta=41.8{}^{\circ}$ , $47.5^{\circ}$ and $62.1^{\circ}$ can be assigned to (100), (101) and (102) planes of carbon (JCPDS No.80-0004) [27, 28], respectively. The diffraction peaks at around $26=44.5^{\circ}$ , $51.8^{\circ}$ and $76.4^{\circ}$ indexed to the (111), (200) and (220) diffractions of metal Ni with face-centered cubic (fcc) structure (JCPDS No.04-0805), respectively, indicating that $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ in MOF precursor have been reduced to Ni particles by reacting with carbon [29]. After melamine participates in the reaction, a broad peak appeared at approximately $2\uptheta=26^{\circ}$ is the (002) plane of graphitic carbon (JCPDS No.41-1487) [30], which is mainly attributed to the presence of CNT. Diffraction peaks of metallic nickel for CNT/Ni@NC composites are bigger than those of Ni/C composite, implying the better crystallinity of cubic metallic nickel.
The relative graphitization degree of carbonaceous materials are discerned by Raman spectra. Two typical peaks located at about 1350 and $1590\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ present the socalled D band and G band that reveals the characteristic of disordered and graphitic carbon, respectively [31]. The intensity ratio $\mathrm{(I_{D}/I_{G})}$ of D and G band can be uses to evaluate the relative graphitization degree of carbon materials [32]. The values of $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ for Ni/C, CNT/Ni@N-C-1, CNT/Ni@N-C-2, and CNT/Ni@N-C-3 composites are calculated to be 1.044, 1.02, 0.9949, and 0.9949, respectively, suggesting the gradually increased graphitization degree.
XPS measurement is conducted to investigate the element valence state of Ni/C and CNT/Ni@N-C composites. The survey spectrum verifies the existence of C, N, and Ni elements in the Ni/C and CNT/Ni@N-C composites (Figure 5). The corresponding relative contents of C, N, and Ni elements calculated based on the peak areas are shown in the table inset of the Figure 5. Obviously, the melamine is important for adjusting the N content. The N content of Ni/C, CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-1, CNT/Ni@N-C-2 and CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-3 composite is $0.75\%$ , $5.34\%$ , $7.58\%$ , and $9.55\%$ , respectively. The N element of Ni/C composite comes from the residual DMF. Figure 6(a1-a4) exhibits the C 1s core-level of Ni/C and CNT/Ni $@$ N-C composites. The three fitting peaks of C 1s for Ni/C composite consists $\mathrm{C=C}$ , C-C, C-O, and O- $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{.C=O}}$ groups at the binding energies of about 283.39, 284.6, 286.03, and $288.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ , respectively [33]. After melamine participates the reaction through pyrolysis, a new peak about at $285~\mathrm{eV}$ , which corresponds to the C-N group, appears in the C 1s fitting curves (Figure 6(a2-a4)) [34]. The N 1s spectrum is resolved into three peaks at about 398, 400.17, and $402\mathrm{eV}$ (Figure 6(b1-b4)) related to the pyridinic N, pyrrolic N and graphitic N, respectively [35], suggesting that N atoms have been incorporated in the carbon substrate. Theoretically, both pyridinic N and pyrrolic N are important to improve the dipolar relaxation losses, and graphitic N is good for improving conduction loss [36]. The relative contents of pyridinic N, pyrrolic N, and graphitic N also calculated based on the peaks areas vary with the mass ratio of melamine and Ni-MOF. The CNT/Ni@N-C-1, CNT/Ni@N-C-2 and CNT/Ni@N-C-3 composite possesses high graphitic N, pyridinic N, and pyrrolic N, respectively. In Figure 6(c1-c4), the XPS spectra of Ni 2p display two shake-up satellites suggesting the weak oxidation of nickel and two primary peaks corresponding to the Ni $2{\tt p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , respectively [24, 37]. Therefore, the N content and state can be easily tuned by adjusting the amount of melamine in the initial precursor.
Figure 6. Curve fitting of C 1s, N 1s and Ni 2p XPS spectra for (a1, b1, c1) Ni/C, (a2, b2, c2) CNT/Ni@N-C-1, (a3, b3, c3) CNT/Ni@N-C-2 and (a4, b4, c4) CNT/Ni@N-C-3 composite.
Both $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ and $\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}$ are the basis for judging the absorbing performance of the wave absorber. The real parts present the storage capability and the imaginary parts are related the dissipation ability of EM energy [38]. As discovered in Figure 7, the content of melamine is vital in modifying the EM parameters of Ni/C composite. From Figure 7(a), the $\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{\prime}}$ values reduce as the frequency increases, which roughly obeys the frequency dispersion behavior [39-42]. This is mainly because the polarization of the electric dipole cannot keep up with the change in the electric field. The $\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}}^{\mathbf{\prime}}$ values of Ni/C composite are enhanced by doping CNTs, which may be caused by the polarization of interfacial and dipole. The $\varepsilon^{\prime}$ values of Ni/C, CNT/Ni@N-C-1, CNT/Ni@N-C-2, and CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-3 change at the range of 8.94-1.32, 20.92-7.41, 19.41-6.28, and 32.58- 11.61, respectively. The $\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{\prime}}$ values of CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-3 are highest among the samples, which suggests the highest energy storage ability. The $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ curves (Figure 7(b)) show similar trend with $\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{\mathbf{\varepsilon}_{\mathbf{\varepsilon}}}^{\prime}$ curves and the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values for CNT/Ni@N-C composites are bigger than those of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}$ composite at 2-12 GHz. At the rest of frequency range $(12\mathrm{-}18\mathrm{GHz})$ , the $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ values of composites follow the order of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}{>}\mathrm{CNT}/\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{N}.\mathrm{C}.1{>}\mathrm{CNT}/\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{N}.$ $C-2>$ CNT/Ni@N-C-3. Therefore, CNTs can significantly change the dielectric constant of Ni/C composite. Besides, the downward tendency for CNT/Ni@N-C composites are more obvious than that of Ni/C composite indicating that the former can produce more electric dipoles [43]. In Figure 7(d), the $\upmu^{\prime}$ values of all composites exhibit decrease $(2{\cdot}6.2\ \mathrm{GHz})$ ) at first, then increase, and last decrease with increasing frequency. It can be observed from Figure 7(e) that the $\upmu^{\prime\prime}$ values for CNT/Ni@N-C composites are higher than those of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}$ composite at the frequency of $9{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ . The $\upmu^{\prime\prime}$ plots present resonance peaks, revealing the existence of natural resonance and exchange resonance. Therefore, the introduction of CNT and $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ has great influence on the EM parameters. The $\mathtt{t a n}\delta_{\mathtt{c}}=\mathtt{\mathtt{c}^{\prime\prime}}\mathtt{\vec{c}^{\prime}}$ and t $\mathrm{an}\delta_{\upmu}=\upmu^{\prime\prime}/\upmu^{\prime}$ values can characterize the dielectric loss and magnetic loss ability of absorbers. Besides, it should be noted that in Figure $7(\mathrm{c},\mathrm{f})$ the values of tan $\delta_{\mathrm{{g}}}$ in 2-11 GHz are larger than $\tan\updelta_{\upmu}$ values, while in high frequency region this phenomenon is contrast, suggesting that the dielectric loss dominates EM attenuation loss mechanism at low frequency and the magnetic loss dominates EM attenuation loss mechanism at high frequency range.
The polarization relaxation process of materials has a great influence on dielectric loss. The relationship of $\mathbf{\varepsilon}^{\mathrm{\prime}}$ and $\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ satisfies the following Formula [44]:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2})^{2}+(\varepsilon^{\prime})^{2}=(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2})^{2}}\end{array}
$$
where $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{s}}$ refers to the static permittivity. $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{\infty}}$ presents relative dielectric permittivity at the high-frequency limit. One semicircle appears in the $\varepsilon^{\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}$ diagram corresponds to a polarization relaxation process, suggesting a Debye relaxation process. As presented in Figure 8, all the composites exhibit multiple distorted semicircles, manifesting the existence of multiple relaxation processes under microwave irradiation. This phenomenon is caused by dipole polarization relaxation and interfacial polarization relaxation [44]. Defects such as doped elements (N and O) in carbon nanotubes and carbon skeletons act as jumping centers to endow electrical dipoles in composites, resulting in stable dipole polarization relaxation. The accumulation of interfacial charges between Ni nanoparticles, C and CNTs lead to interfacial polarization. The straight line at the end of Cole-Cole curves is related to the conduction loss that mainly comes from C, CNTs and Ni particles.
The magnetic loss is also important for absorbing the EM waves. Generally, the magnetic loss mainly comes from domain wall resonance, hysteresis loss, eddy current loss, and natural/exchange resonance [45]. Domain wall resonance loss and hysteresis loss always occur at low frequency [46]. As for eddy current loss, it can be estimated by $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{0}=\upmu^{\prime\prime}(\upmu^{\prime})^{-2}}f^{-1}$ . If the values of $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ keep constant with the increase of frequency, the eddy current loss contributes to magnetic loss. Contrarily, if the values of $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ change a lot, the magnetic loss mainly originates from natural resonance and exchange resonance [47]. The values of $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ for all composites are investigated in Figure 9. Obviously, $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ curves of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}$ composite fluctuate at the frequency of 2-11 GHz, whereas it almost keep content at 11-18 GHz, which means the natural resonance, exchange resonance, and eddy current loss dominate the magnetic loss. $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ curves of CNT/Ni@N-C composites fluctuate over the entire frequency range, manifesting that natural resonance and the exchange resonance dominate the magnetic loss.
The RL values are defined by the following equations [22]:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{RL}(d\mathbf{B})=20\log\left|\frac{Z_{i n}-Z_{0}}{Z_{i n}+Z_{0}}\right|}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{Z_{\mathrm{in}}=Z_{0}\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}\mathrm{tanh}\left(j\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\sqrt{\mu_{r}\varepsilon_{r}}\right)}\end{array}
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ denotes the normalized input impedance and $Z_{0}$ is the impedance of free space, respectively, $f$ is the EM frequency, $d$ is the thickness of the absorber, c is $3\mathrm{~x~}10^{8}\mathrm{m/s}$ . Particularly, when the $\mathrm{RL}\leq-10~\mathrm{dB}$ , $90\%$ of incident EM wave will be attenuation. Figure 10 exhibits three dimension and two dimension curves of RL for simples at 1.5- $4.0\mathrm{mm}$ . The $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of Ni/C composite is -21.7 dB at $8.6\mathrm{GHz}$ when the matching thickness is $4.0\ \mathrm{mm}$ (Figure 10(a, e, i)). For the CNT/Ni@N-C-1 composite (Figure 10(b, f, j)), the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of -24.1 dB at $2.5~\mathrm{mm}$ is obtained at 10.9 GHz. Besides, when the thickness is $1.7\ \mathrm{mm}$ , the superior EAB is $5.7~\mathrm{GHz}$ (12.3-18 GHz) at the corresponding $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of -21.3 dB (Figure 10(f)). As for CNT/Ni@N-C-2 composite, the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value is -55.1 dB at 10.56 GHz with corresponding thickness of $2.5\mathrm{mm}$ (Figure 10(c, g, k)). Moreover, by changing the thickness from $1.5\mathrm{mm}$ to 4.0 mm, the EAB reaches 11.2 GHz (6.0-17.2 GHz). However, the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-3 composite is only -17.5 dB at $13.6\:\mathrm{GHz}$ with a thickness of $1.5\mathrm{mm}$ (Figure 10(d, h, l)). Overall, the appropriate amount CNT and nitrogen-doped can effectively improve the EM wave absorption properties of Ni/C composites.
C-1, (c, g, k) CNT/Ni@N-C-2, (d, h, l) CNT/Ni@N-C-3 composites.
In order to better understand the difference of EM wave absorption performance for absorbers, the attenuation and impedance matching are analyzed. The attenuation constant $(\upalpha)$ is used to reveal the microwave attenuation ability of materials and it is calculated by the formula (4) [24]:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\alpha=\frac{\pi f\sqrt{2}}{c}\sqrt{\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}-\varepsilon^{\prime}\mu^{\prime}\right)+\sqrt{\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\mu^{\prime\prime}-\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}+\mu^{\prime\prime}\varepsilon^{\prime}\right)^{2}}}}\end{array}
$$
Figure 11(a) presents the $\mathfrak{a}$ of $\mathrm{Ni}/\mathrm{C}$ and CNT/Ni $\textcircled{a}\mathrm{N-C}$ composites. The $\mathfrak{a}$ value of all the composites display the increasing trend when the frequency increases from $2\mathrm{GHz}$ to $18~\mathrm{{GHz}}$ and follows the sequence of $\alpha(\mathrm{CNT/Ni}@\mathrm{N-C-3})>\alpha(\mathrm{CNT/Ni}@\mathrm{N-C-1})>$ $\mathrm{{a}}(\mathrm{{CNT/Ni}}@\mathrm{{N-C-2}})>\mathrm{{a}}(\mathrm{{Ni}}/\mathrm{{C}})$ . This result is different from RL analysis result. Hence, the impendence matching together with moderate attenuation capability may endow the resultant composite better microwave absorption performance. Impedance matching is important to determine the EM absorption properties. When the surface resistance of absorber is close to the resistance of free space $\mathrm{\langle}Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}|=1\$ ), the external EM wave penetrate the absorber. Figure 11(b) compares the $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert$ values of Ni/C and CNT/Ni@N-C composites with a constant sample thickness of $2.5~\mathrm{mm}$ . Apparently, the $\lvert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\rvert$ values of CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-2 composite is closest to 1, which indicates that the CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-2 composite has the best impedance matching compared with other composites. Accordingly, the $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ of -55.1 dB is achieved for CNT/Ni@N-C-2 composite at the same frequency when $\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert=1.0$ , which satisfies the near zero reflection condition. Combining the impedance matching and attenuation ability, CNT/Ni@N-C-2 composite possess the superior EM wave absorbing performance.
According to the above analysis, the feasible EM wave absorption mechanism of CNT/Ni@N-C composite could be attributed to the following aspects, as shown in Scheme 2. Firstly, the suitable impedance matching and microwave attenuation ensures more EM waves enter the material and then be efficiently attenuated. The combination of $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{N-C}$ and CNT improves the microwave absorption performance of composite. Secondly, the accumulation of space charges at multiple interfaces among paraffin, Ni nanoparticles, carbon, and CNTs cause interface polarization. Besides, these heterogeneous interfaces also facilitate multiple scattering and reflection of incident EM waves [53]. Thirdly, Ni nanoparticles provide magnetic loss by natural resonance and the exchange resonance. Ni nanoparticles embedded in N-C increase the dielectric loss by enhancing the interfacial polarization [54]. And the CNTs catalyzed by Ni particles interconnect to conductive network, which is beneficial to the improvement of the conduction loss and provides an effective way for electronic hopping and migration. Finally, plentiful functional groups and N doping in CNT/Ni@N-C composites generate amount of dipoles, which act as polarized centers to attenuate incident EM waves [55]. Therefore, the excellent microwave absorption ability of the CNT/Ni@NC composite is ascribed to the good impedance matching as well as the synergistic effect of magnetic and dielectric loss.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, CNT/Ni@N-C composites were prepared via calcining the mixture of Ni-MOF microspheres and melamine through two-stage programmed heating process, which does not require complicated chemical treatment to combine Ni/C composite with CNTs. Meanwhile, each CNT tip is capped with $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{C}$ core-shell particle, and the spherical Ni/C composite is composed of many $\mathrm{Ni}@\mathrm{C}$ core-shell particles, which reduce the oxidation or corrosion by air or acid. The amount of melamine can effectively tune the N content and N dopant state in the composite. The as-synthesized CNT/Ni@N-C-1 composite with high content of graphitic N displays broad EAB of 5.7 GHz (12.3-18 GHz) at the corresponding $\mathrm{RL}_{\mathrm{max}}$ value of -21.3 dB with $1.7~\mathrm{mm}$ . The as-synthesized CNT/Ni $@$ N-C-2 composite with high content of pyridinic N presents strong EM wave absorption of -55.1 dB with thickness of $2.5\mathrm{mm}$ and wide EAB 11.2 GHz (6.0-17.2 GHz) as the thickness varies from $1.5~\mathrm{mm}$ to 4.0 mm. This work is expected to pave a way to construct novel materials for EM wave absorption.
